**Read:** [Intermediate Fluid Mechanics — James Liburdy](../sources/Intermediate-Fluid-Mechanics-Liburdy.lingua#r_45KKWKB7DTN9MMQ951P62JHH5N), chapters IV and V in full; [Fluid Mechanics — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V), two-dimensional flow and complex velocity potential; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 2 potential motion.

For an irrotational incompressible region, write u=∇φ and solve ∇²φ=0. In two dimensions an appropriate streamfunction describes the same divergence-free motion, and complex-variable methods connect the two. Superpose uniform flows, sources, sinks, doublets and vortices only with their actual singularities and boundary conditions in view.

Construct flow around a circular cylinder and obtain surface pressure from a correctly normalized Bernoulli relation. The steady inviscid noncirculatory solution predicts zero drag. This is a consequence of the ideal model, not evidence that a real body has no resistance. Boundary layers, separation and wake momentum explain why the model's missing physics matters.

Solve both a velocity-potential and a streamfunction version of one problem. Identify excluded singularities and the far-field condition. Check no penetration, net flux, circulation and symmetry before plotting streamlines. A visually attractive streamline plot can conceal a source placed inside the fluid or an incorrect boundary sign.

**Continue:** [Circulation, lift and the viscous selection of a flow](Circulation-lift-and-the-viscous-selection-of-a-flow.lingua#r_16GKKW380EKZDXK81MZ78J0VB6).

